 of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/state.json" "file_sha256" "906f678810dd5b5f936f3f2c6dfb2dbc42bb210083ecd19c72237dfeb6d349a6" "basis" 1362 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1debe7e369313b0c41c861b64b81871d063dd14496175503d40af884afe6a06b" "basis" 1362 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039394/edb-import/verif