denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e97d9705bd025961efbe1f184f318771247c1647f5c8bc45b22bf6d856a58fd"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-9263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,-2),(-3,2),(2,2),$ and $(-1,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-5,-8)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-(-2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(0,-2),(-5,-8),(0,2),(0,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e97d9705bd025961efbe1f184f318771247c1647f5c8bc45b22bf6d856a58fd"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-11974" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{18-16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e97d9705bd025961efbe1f184f318771247c1647f5c8bc45b22bf6d856a58fd"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-85082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(2)(7)-(3)(5)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e97d9705bd025961efbe1f184f318771247c1647f5c8bc45b22bf6d856a58fd"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317436" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(1)(-3)-(2)(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-3+2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e97d9705bd025961efbe1f184f318771247c1647f5c8bc45b22bf6d856a58fd"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"} {"question" "q-317512" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073528/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fffcee9aa19a9baf1e343749c259b90887b2d8a88b18c824964e8dc9422aa5b"}] "usage" {"q-9263" [] "q-317436" []} "basis" 2697 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186283845 17592186283847 17592186292368 17592186292369 17592186292370 1759